##* {{RQ:King James Version|Deuteronomy|14|26|page=286|column=1|passage=And thou ſhalt '''beſtow''' that money for whatſoeuer thy ſoule lutſeth after, for oxen, or for ſheepe, or for wine, or for ſtrong drinke, or for whatſoeuer thy ſoule deſireth: and thou ſhalt eat there before the {{smallcaps|Lord}} thy God, and thou ſhalt reioyce, thou and thine houſhold.}}
# To [[impart]] (something) [[gratuitously]]; to [[present#Verb|present]] (something) to someone or something, especially as a [[gift#Noun|gift]] or an [[honour#Noun|honour]]; to [[confer]], to [[give#Verb|give]].
#: {{ux|en|Medals were '''bestowed''' on the winning team.}}
#* {{RQ:Coverdale Bible|book=2 Chronicles|chapter=24|verse=7|folio=c|column=2|passage=For y&#868; vngodly [[w:Athaliah|Athalia]] & hir ſonnes haue waiſted the houſe of God: and all that was halowed for the houſe of the LORDE, haue they '''beſtowed''' on [[w:Baal|Baalim]].}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Titus Andronicus Q1|act=IV|scene=ii|page=65|passage=Harke yee Lords, you ſee I haue giuen her Phiſicke, / And you muſt needs '''beſtovv''' her Funerall, {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Raleigh Historie of the World|book=2|chapter=Of the Time of the Birth of Abraham: And of the Use of This Question, for the Ordering of the Storie of the Assyrian Empire|section=III (The Answere to One of the Obiections Proposed, Shewing that Abraham Made but One Iourney out of Mesopotamia into Canaan: And It, after His Fathers Death)|page=222|passage=[H]e &#91;{{w|Moses}}&#93; '''beſtovveth''' on the ſtory of ''{{w|Abraham}}'' fourteene chapters, beginning vvith his birth in the eleuenth, and ending vvith his death in the fiue and tvventieth; and this time endured but 175. yeares.}}
#* {{RQ:Bunyan Pilgrim's Progress|page=195|passage=Lord, I have heard that thou art a merciful God, and haſt ordained that thy Son [[w:Jesus|Jeſus Chriſt]] ſhould be the Saviour of the VVorld, and moreover, that thou art vvilling to '''beſtovv''' him upon ſuch a poor ſinner as I am, {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Johnson Rambler|issue=38|date=28 July 1750|page=94|passage=I am come to offer you gifts, vvhich only your ovvn folly can make vain. You here pray for vvater, and vvater vvill I '''beſtovv'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Mary Shelley Frankenstein|volume=II|chapter=VIII|pages=132–133|pageref=133|passage=Soft tears again bedewed my cheeks, and I even raised my humid eyes with thankfulness towards the blessed sun which '''bestowed''' such joy upon me.}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Christmas Carol|page=11|passage=He stopped at the outer door to '''bestow''' the greetings of the season on the clerk, who, cold as he was, was warmer than Scrooge; for he returned them cordially.}}
#* {{RQ:Hector Wooing O't|volume=II|chapter=VII|page=168|passage=Sometimes I am caught by a delightful fragment in a magazine, and blaze up into the fiercest interest, '''bestow''' maledictions on the delay which the intervening month creates, but am burnt out by the time it expires, and so lose the thread.}}
#* {{RQ:Lindsay Redheap|edition=US|chapter=4|section=III|page=86|passage=Do they [parents] not sneakingly '''bestow''' on me their crass inability to do anything with their own misbegotten progeny, a subterfuge which I scornfully fub off on text-books?}}
#* {{quote-web|en|author=Illiad [pseudonym; J. D. Frazer]|title=The Large Hadron Collider Game: Or ‘Why Science is Hard and Getting People to Fund It is Harder’|genre=webcomic|work=w:User Friendly|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20220225175607/https://ars.userfriendly.org/cartoons/?id=20080928|date=28 September 2008|passage=CERN '''bestows''' slush fund on the LHC. Take all pennies from the CERN space.}}
