## To [[place#Verb|place]] or [[put#Verb|put]] (someone or something) somewhere or in a certain [[situation]]; to [[dispose#Verb|dispose]] of.
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merry Wives Q1|act=IV|scene=v|page=55|passage=The diuell take the one partie, / And his dam the other, / And theyle be both '''beſtovved'''. / ''I'' haue endured more for their ſakes, / Then man is able to endure.|footer=That is, the two wives will be placed in hell by the Devil and his wife.}}
##* {{RQ:Guillim Heraldrie|chapter=To the Courteous Reader|page=20|passage=Moreover I haue {{...}} '''beſtovved''' the chiefe grounds, ''Principles'', ''Rules'', and ''Obſeruations'' [of heraldry] vnder their proper heads, and manifeſted their vſe by examples of ſpeciall choice, {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Middleton Works|volume=V|play=Widow|act=I|scene=i|lines=65–68|page=128|passage=Here are blank warrants of all dispositions; give me but the name and nature of your malefactor, and I'll '''bestow''' him according to his merits.}}
##* {{RQ:Pope Iliad|volume=III|book=IX|page=16|lines=283–284|passage=VVith Bread the glitt’ring Caniſters they load, / VVhich round the Board ''Menætius''’s Son '''beſtovv’d'''; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Robert Browning Red Cotton|page=116|passage=The white domestic pigeon pairs secure, / Nay, does mere duty by '''bestowing''' egg / In authorized compartment, warm and safe, / Boarding about, and gilded spire above, / Hoisted on pole, to dogs' and cats' despair!}}
## To [[deposit#Verb|deposit]] (something) for [[safekeeping]]; to [[lay up]] (something) in [[store#Noun|store]]; to [[stow#Verb|stow]].
##* {{RQ:Tyndale NT|format=full|Luke|12|16|folio=xcvij|passage=The [[lands|londes]] of a certayne man brought forth frutes plenteouſly&#11805; and he thought in hym ſilfe ſayinge: whatt ſhall I do&#11805; becauſe I have noo roume where to '''beſtowe''' my frutes?}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Comedy of Errors|act=I|scene=ii|page=87|column=1|passage=Novv as I am a Chriſtian anſvver me, / In vvhat ſafe place you haue '''beſtovv'd''' my monie; / Or I ſhall breake that merrie ſconce of yours / That ſtands on tricks, vvhen I am vndiſpos'd: / VVhere is the thouſand Markes thou hadſt of me?}}
##* {{RQ:Pliny Holland Historie of the World|tome=2|book=XXXVI|chapter=Of Certaine Stones which will Quickly Consume the Bodies that be Laid therein. Of Others Againe that Preserve Them a Long Time. Of the Stone Called Assius, and the Medicinable Properties thereof|page=587|passage=Near unto Aſſos, a citie in Troas, there is found in the quarries a certaine ſtone called Sarcophagus, {{...}} The reaſon of the name is this, becauſe that vvithin the ſpace of fortie daies it is knovvne for certain to conſume the bodies of the dead vvhich are '''beſtovved''' therein, skin, fleſh, and bone, all ſave the teeth.}}
##* {{RQ:Livy Holland Romane Historie|year=1659|book=I|page=6|passage=But as ſome of the Oxen in driving, miſſed their fellovvs behind and honing after them, bellovved as their nature is: ''{{w|Hercules}}'' chanced to heare them lovv again, and anſvver from out of the cave vvherein they had been '''beſtovved''': vvhereat he turned back, and made haſte thither.}}
##* {{RQ:Tolkien Silmarillion|page=298|passage=Of the Three Rings that the Elves had preserved unsullied no open word was ever spoken among the Wise, and few even of the Eldar knew where they were '''bestowed'''.}}
## {{lb|en|also|reflexive}} To [[provide]] (someone or oneself) with [[accommodation]]; to [[find#Verb|find]] [[quarters#Noun|quarters]] for (someone or oneself); to [[lodge#Verb|lodge]], to [[quarter#Verb|quarter]].
